Task-Specific:
- Reproduce includes correctly: `.. include::` injects lines via Docutils `state_machine.insert_input()` and bypasses `SphinxStandaloneReader.read_source()`, so `source-read` won’t fire unless you hook include-time reading.
- Don’t “fix” this by monkeypatching `state_machine.insert_input()`; it’s brittle and can corrupt source/line bookkeeping, relative-path behavior inside included content, and nested parsing (confirmed regression).
- Avoid large copy/paste reimplementations of Docutils’ `Include.run()` inside Sphinx; it’s a maintenance hazard and easy to drift from Docutils semantics (start/end slicing, encoding, include_log, dependency tracking, parser options, literal/code modes).
- If overriding Sphinx’s `Include` directive, keep the override minimal and preserve Docutils behaviors:
  - Respect `start-line/end-line/start-after/end-before`, `encoding`, `tab-width`
  - Preserve circular-include detection (`document.include_log`)
  - Preserve `:literal:` / `:code:` / `:parser:` behavior (often safest to delegate those paths to Docutils)
  - Insert with correct `source` metadata and offsets so warnings, `image::` resolution, etc. behave as before.
- Path handling must be consistent: keep absolute path for reading; don’t join a base dir with an absolute path; pass a stable, correct “source” string to Docutils for location tracking.
- Event semantics: don’t invent misleading `docname`s from include filenames. If reusing `source-read`, define a clear rule (typically: use the including document’s `env.docname`; include path should be conveyed separately if needed). Consider a dedicated include-focused event if API change is allowed.
- Test expectations should guard against regressions: beyond “string replaced”, also ensure included content still parses correctly and relative references from the included file (e.g., images) still resolve.

Repo-Specific:
- Test roots must be named `tests/roots/test-<name>` and referenced as `@pytest.mark.sphinx(testroot='<name>')`; wrong root naming wastes runs.
